{
  "title": "Series and DataFrame",
  "summary": "pandas' two building blocks: a labelled column and a table of them.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Series: an array with labels",
      "body": [
        "A pandas **Series** is a NumPy array plus an **index**: a label for every value.",
        "",
        "```python",
        "import pandas as pd",
        "",
        "stock = pd.Series([12, 0, 7], index=[\"apples\", \"pears\", \"plums\"])",
        "stock[\"plums\"]          # 7      look up by label",
        "stock * 2               # vectorised, like NumPy",
        "stock[stock > 5]        # masks work too",
        "pd.Series({\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2})   # from a dict: keys become the index",
        "```",
        "",
        "Labels matter because arithmetic **aligns by label**, not position: adding two Series",
        "matches `\"apples\"` with `\"apples\"`. A label present in only one side gives `NaN`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "import pandas as pd",
        "",
        "jan = pd.Series({\"apples\": 10, \"pears\": 4})",
        "feb = pd.Series({\"pears\": 6, \"apples\": 1, \"plums\": 3})",
        "total = jan + feb",
        "print(total[\"apples\"], total[\"pears\"])",
        "print(total.isna().sum())",
        "print(total.index.tolist())"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "11.0 10.0",
        "1",
        "['apples', 'pears', 'plums']"
      ],
      "explain": "Values are matched by label, whatever their order. `plums` is only in `feb`, so its total is NaN. The result's index is sorted union of both."
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "DataFrame: a table of Series",
      "body": [
        "A **DataFrame** is a table: named columns that share one row index. Each column is a Series",
        "with its own dtype.",
        "",
        "```python",
        "df = pd.DataFrame({",
        "    \"product\": [\"Widget\", \"Gadget\", \"Gizmo\"],",
        "    \"qty\": [3, 1, 2],",
        "    \"price\": [2.5, 10.0, 7.25],",
        "})",
        "df.shape            # (3, 3)   rows, columns",
        "df.columns.tolist() # ['product', 'qty', 'price']",
        "df[\"qty\"]           # one column: a Series",
        "df[\"qty\"].sum()     # 6",
        "```",
        "",
        "A dict of lists (one list per column) is the quickest way to make a small table; a list of",
        "dicts (one dict per row) also works."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "`df` is a DataFrame. What is `type(df[\"price\"])`?",
      "options": [
        "`pandas.Series`",
        "`pandas.DataFrame`",
        "`list`",
        "`numpy.ndarray`"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explain": "One column is a Series. `df[[\"price\"]]` (a list of names) gives a one-column DataFrame."
    },
    {
      "type": "match",
      "prompt": "Match each attribute to what it tells you.",
      "pairs": [
        [
          "`df.shape`",
          "(number of rows, number of columns)"
        ],
        [
          "`df.columns`",
          "the column names"
        ],
        [
          "`df.index`",
          "the row labels"
        ],
        [
          "`df.dtypes`",
          "the type of each column"
        ],
        [
          "`len(df)`",
          "the number of rows"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "import pandas as pd",
        "",
        "df = pd.DataFrame([",
        "    {\"city\": \"Oslo\", \"temp\": 4},",
        "    {\"city\": \"Rome\", \"temp\": 18},",
        "    {\"city\": \"Lima\", \"temp\": 22, \"rain\": 0.0},",
        "])",
        "print(df.shape)",
        "print(df.columns.tolist())",
        "print(int(df[\"temp\"].max()))",
        "print(int(df[\"rain\"].isna().sum()))"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "(3, 3)",
        "['city', 'temp', 'rain']",
        "22",
        "2"
      ],
      "explain": "From a list of dicts, pandas makes the union of all keys the columns; rows missing a key get NaN."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "Build a table",
      "prompt": [
        "Write `make_inventory()` that returns this DataFrame, with columns in this order and the",
        "default index `0, 1, 2`:",
        "",
        "| item | qty | price |",
        "|---|---|---|",
        "| pen | 10 | 1.5 |",
        "| pad | 4 | 3.0 |",
        "| ink | 0 | 12.0 |"
      ],
      "starter": "import pandas as pd\n\n\ndef make_inventory():\n    pass\n",
      "solution": [
        "import pandas as pd",
        "",
        "",
        "def make_inventory():",
        "    return pd.DataFrame({",
        "        \"item\": [\"pen\", \"pad\", \"ink\"],",
        "        \"qty\": [10, 4, 0],",
        "        \"price\": [1.5, 3.0, 12.0],",
        "    })"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "`pd.DataFrame({...})` with one list per column.",
        "Keys in the dict become the columns, in order."
      ],
      "template": [
        "{{CODE}}",
        "import pandas as _pd",
        "_df = make_inventory()",
        "Dojo.check(\"returns a DataFrame\", isinstance(_df, _pd.DataFrame), \"got \" + type(_df).__name__)",
        "Dojo.eq(\"columns\", [\"item\", \"qty\", \"price\"], list(_df.columns))",
        "Dojo.eq(\"shape\", (3, 3), _df.shape)",
        "Dojo.eq(\"item column\", [\"pen\", \"pad\", \"ink\"], _df[\"item\"].tolist())",
        "Dojo.eq(\"qty column\", [10, 4, 0], _df[\"qty\"].tolist())",
        "Dojo.eq(\"price column\", [1.5, 3.0, 12.0], _df[\"price\"].tolist())"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "blanks",
      "prompt": "Build a Series from a dict and keep the big values.",
      "code": [
        "import pandas as pd",
        "",
        "pop = pd.__1__({\"Oslo\": 0.7, \"Rome\": 2.8, \"Lima\": 10.0})",
        "big = pop[pop __2__ 1]",
        "print(big.__3__.tolist())"
      ],
      "blanks": [
        "Series",
        ">",
        "index"
      ],
      "bank": [
        "DataFrame",
        "<",
        "columns",
        "values"
      ],
      "explain": "Prints `['Rome', 'Lima']`: the labels of the values above 1."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "Price changes",
      "prompt": [
        "Write `price_change(old, new)`: both are Series of prices indexed by product name, but",
        "products may be missing from either. Return a Series of the **percentage change**",
        "`(new - old) / old * 100`, rounded to 1 decimal, containing **only products present in both**.",
        "",
        "```python",
        "old = pd.Series({\"tea\": 2.0, \"milk\": 1.0, \"jam\": 3.0})",
        "new = pd.Series({\"milk\": 1.2, \"tea\": 1.5, \"bread\": 2.0})",
        "price_change(old, new)   # milk 20.0, tea -25.0",
        "```",
        "",
        "Let label alignment do the matching, then drop the NaNs with `.dropna()`."
      ],
      "starter": "import pandas as pd\n\n\ndef price_change(old, new):\n    pass\n",
      "solution": [
        "import pandas as pd",
        "",
        "",
        "def price_change(old, new):",
        "    change = (new - old) / old * 100",
        "    return change.dropna().round(1)"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "`(new - old) / old * 100` already matches products by label.",
        "`.dropna().round(1)`"
      ],
      "template": [
        "{{CODE}}",
        "import pandas as _pd",
        "_r = price_change(_pd.Series({\"tea\": 2.0, \"milk\": 1.0, \"jam\": 3.0}), _pd.Series({\"milk\": 1.2, \"tea\": 1.5, \"bread\": 2.0}))",
        "Dojo.check(\"returns a Series\", isinstance(_r, _pd.Series), \"got \" + type(_r).__name__)",
        "Dojo.eq(\"only shared products, rounded\", {\"milk\": 20.0, \"tea\": -25.0}, _r.to_dict())",
        "Dojo.eq(\"nothing shared\", {}, price_change(_pd.Series({\"a\": 1.0}), _pd.Series({\"b\": 1.0})).to_dict())"
      ],
      "forbid": [
        {
          "pattern": "\\bfor\\b",
          "message": "No loop: let the labels align"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "Inventory value",
      "prompt": [
        "Write `inventory_value(items, qty, price)` taking three lists of the same length. Return a",
        "tuple `(df, total)`:",
        "",
        "- `df` is a DataFrame with columns `item`, `qty`, `price` and a new column `value`",
        "  (`qty * price`),",
        "- `total` is the sum of `value` as a plain `float`.",
        "",
        "```python",
        "df, total = inventory_value([\"pen\", \"pad\"], [10, 4], [1.5, 3.0])",
        "df[\"value\"].tolist()   # [15.0, 12.0]",
        "total                  # 27.0",
        "```"
      ],
      "starter": "import pandas as pd\n\n\ndef inventory_value(items, qty, price):\n    pass\n",
      "solution": [
        "import pandas as pd",
        "",
        "",
        "def inventory_value(items, qty, price):",
        "    df = pd.DataFrame({\"item\": items, \"qty\": qty, \"price\": price})",
        "    df[\"value\"] = df[\"qty\"] * df[\"price\"]",
        "    return df, float(df[\"value\"].sum())"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Build the frame from a dict of the three lists.",
        "`df[\"value\"] = df[\"qty\"] * df[\"price\"]`, then `float(df[\"value\"].sum())`."
      ],
      "template": [
        "{{CODE}}",
        "_df, _total = inventory_value([\"pen\", \"pad\"], [10, 4], [1.5, 3.0])",
        "Dojo.eq(\"columns\", [\"item\", \"qty\", \"price\", \"value\"], list(_df.columns))",
        "Dojo.eq(\"value column\", [15.0, 12.0], _df[\"value\"].tolist())",
        "Dojo.eq(\"total\", 27.0, _total)",
        "Dojo.check(\"total is a float\", isinstance(_total, float), \"got \" + type(_total).__name__)",
        "_df2, _t2 = inventory_value([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"], [0, 2, 1], [9.0, 0.25, 4.0])",
        "Dojo.eq(\"three items\", (3, 4, 4.5), (_df2.shape[0], _df2.shape[1], _t2))"
      ],
      "forbid": [
        {
          "pattern": "\\bfor\\b",
          "message": "No loop"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
